# Theory — Design Patterns in OOP

## 1. Factory Method

Separates *what to create* from *how to create it*. The caller asks for a product; the factory decides which concrete type to instantiate:

```cpp
struct Logger {
    virtual ~Logger() = default;
    virtual void log(std::string_view msg) = 0;
};

struct ConsoleLogger : Logger {
    void log(std::string_view msg) override {
        std::cout << "[console] " << msg << "\n";
    }
};

// Factory function — caller doesn't know the concrete type
std::unique_ptr<Logger> makeLogger(std::string_view type) {
    if (type == "console") return std::make_unique<ConsoleLogger>();
    throw std::invalid_argument("unknown logger type");
}
```

**When to use**: When you need to decouple object creation from the code that uses the object. Avoids hard-coding `new ConcreteClass` throughout the codebase.

## 2. Strategy

Defines a family of algorithms, encapsulates each one, and makes them interchangeable. In modern C++ this is often done with `std::function`:

```cpp
class Sorter {
public:
    using Strategy = std::function<void(std::vector<int>&)>;
    void setStrategy(Strategy s) { strategy_ = std::move(s); }
    void sort(std::vector<int>& data) { strategy_(data); }
private:
    Strategy strategy_;
};

// Swap algorithms at runtime:
sorter.setStrategy([](auto& v){ std::sort(v.begin(), v.end()); });
sorter.setStrategy([](auto& v){ std::stable_sort(v.begin(), v.end()); });
```

**When to use**: When you have multiple variants of an algorithm and want to select at runtime, or want to isolate algorithm details from the context.

## 3. Observer

Defines a one-to-many dependency: when one object changes state, all dependents are notified automatically.

```cpp
struct EventListener {
    virtual ~EventListener() = default;
    virtual void onEvent(std::string_view event) = 0;
};

class EventBus {
public:
    void subscribe(EventListener* l)   { listeners_.push_back(l); }
    void unsubscribe(EventListener* l) {
        std::erase(listeners_, l);
    }
    void publish(std::string_view event) {
        for (auto* l : listeners_) l->onEvent(event);
    }
private:
    std::vector<EventListener*> listeners_;  // non-owning
};
```

**When to use**: UI event systems, logging pipelines, reactive state management.

## 4. Builder

Constructs a complex object step-by-step, separating construction from representation:

```cpp
struct HttpRequest {
    std::string url, method, body;
    std::map<std::string, std::string> headers;

    struct Builder {
        HttpRequest r;
        Builder& url(std::string v)    { r.url = std::move(v); return *this; }
        Builder& method(std::string v) { r.method = std::move(v); return *this; }
        Builder& header(std::string k, std::string v) { r.headers[k] = v; return *this; }
        HttpRequest build() { return std::move(r); }
    };
};

auto req = HttpRequest::Builder{}
    .url("https://api.example.com/data")
    .method("GET")
    .header("Accept", "application/json")
    .build();
```

## 5. Modern C++ Shortcuts

Many classic patterns are unnecessary in modern C++:

| Classic Pattern | Modern Replacement |
|----------------|-------------------|
| Command | `std::function` + lambda |
| Strategy | `std::function` |
| Singleton | `static` local variable (Meyer's Singleton) |
| Template Method | Variadic templates or `std::function` hook |

## Self-Check

- When is a pattern *overkill*? (Simple = better)
- How does `std::function` replace the classic Command pattern?
- What's the difference between Factory Method and Abstract Factory?

