# Pitfalls — Design Patterns in OOP

## ❌ Pitfall 1: Pattern Overuse — "Golden Hammer"

Applying patterns to simple problems adds unnecessary complexity:

```cpp
// ❌ Overkill for a single logger
class LoggerFactory {
    static std::unique_ptr<Logger> create();
};
class LoggerBuilder { ... };
class LoggerRegistry { ... };

// ✅ Just construct it
auto logger = std::make_unique<ConsoleLogger>();
```

Patterns exist to solve **specific problems** — not to make code look sophisticated.

## ❌ Pitfall 2: Singleton Everywhere

Singleton is the most abused pattern. It creates global mutable state, which makes code:
- Hard to test (can't inject mock)
- Non-thread-safe by default
- Tightly coupled to one global instance

```cpp
// ❌ Global mutable singleton
class Config {
    static Config& instance() { static Config c; return c; }
    // Shared by all code → impossible to test in isolation
};

// ✅ Dependency injection — pass it where needed
void processData(const Config& cfg, DataSource& src);
```

## ❌ Pitfall 3: Observer Memory Leaks — Dangling Listeners

```cpp
void setupListeners(EventBus& bus) {
    auto listener = std::make_unique<MyListener>();
    bus.subscribe(listener.get());  // ❌ listener destroyed at end of scope!
    // bus holds a dangling pointer
}
```

Fix: Use `weak_ptr` observers, or ensure listeners outlive the bus, or unsubscribe in the destructor.

## ❌ Pitfall 4: Virtual Dispatch Overhead in Hot Paths

Strategy via virtual functions has per-call overhead. In hot loops, prefer `std::function` with known lambdas, or templates:

```cpp
// ❌ Virtual call in tight loop
for (auto& item : million_items) strategy_->process(item);

// ✅ Template (zero overhead if inlined)
template<typename Fn>
void processAll(const Fn& fn, const std::vector<Item>& items) {
    for (const auto& item : items) fn(item);
}
```

## Prevention Checklist

- Apply a pattern only when you see the specific *problem* it solves
- Prefer `std::function` + lambdas over full class hierarchies for simple strategies/commands
- Avoid Singleton; prefer dependency injection
- Ensure Observer listeners are properly unsubscribed before destruction
- Profile before optimizing virtual dispatch

